# Cloudflare Workers Static Assets — `_headers` file (Netlify-compatible).
#
# Vite copies this file verbatim out of `public/` into the client output
# directory during `pnpm run build`, where Cloudflare Workers Static
# Assets picks it up at deploy time and applies the listed response
# headers to every matching request. `pnpm exec vite preview` parses the
# same file and reports `Parsed N valid header rules`, so the rules below
# are observable locally as well as in production.
#
# Pattern syntax: Cloudflare's Static Assets uses Express-style named
# placeholders for path parameters, e.g. `/tools/:slug/app/*`. The
# Netlify-style `*` wildcard is restricted to one per rule, so we use
# `:slug` for the Tool slug and `*` only for the trailing path.
# Reference:
#   https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/headers/
#
# Scope limit (Issue #291): `_headers` governs STATIC ASSETS ONLY.
# Cloudflare documents that "Custom headers defined in the `_headers`
# file are not applied to responses generated by your Worker code, even
# if the request URL matches a rule." The `/` HTML is streamed by the
# TanStack Start SSR handler inside the Worker, so its Cache-Control is
# set in `src/server.ts` instead of here.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORS — Tool iframes
#
# Tool iframes are sandboxed `allow-scripts` only (no
# `allow-same-origin`). The iframe document therefore has an opaque
# origin, and any `<script type="module">` and `<link rel="stylesheet">`
# inside it becomes a CORS request — module scripts are always fetched
# with CORS, even without the `crossorigin` attribute. The default
# Static Assets response does not include `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`,
# so the browser blocks the bundle and React never mounts.
#
# We scope the wildcard CORS to `/tools/:slug/app/*` only — the
# namespacing guarantees these are same-origin Tool artefacts emitted
# by the Tool build orchestrator (scripts/build-tools.mjs). The host's
# own `/assets/*` keeps its default behaviour (same-origin module
# scripts from the host page, no CORS).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Inbound embed policy — Tool artefacts (Issue #448)
#
# `frame-ancestors` answers "who may FRAME this document". That is a
# different question from the CORS question above ("who may READ it"),
# and a different question again from the host's OUTBOUND policy ("which
# Tools may this site embed, and under what sandbox" — owned by
# `src/tools/registry.ts` and the manifest's `delivery.iframe`). The
# three are deliberately not derived from each other.
#
# `'self'` is the value that keeps the existing Tool iframes working: the
# only ancestor of `/tools/<slug>/app/index.html` is our own shell at
# `/tools/<slug>`, same origin, so `'self'` permits it. A third party
# embedding a Tool document directly is denied. The shell itself is a
# Worker response, so it is covered by the sibling policy in
# `src/server.ts` (also `'self'` on public paths, `'none'` on Admin /
# Account) — nothing that works today stops working.
#
# The rule that matters is `/tools/:slug/app/*`, because the entry
# document is `<artifact_path>/index.html` and the `:slug/app` pattern
# matches only the bare path. Both rules carry the header so the whole
# Tool namespace is covered whichever one a future entry HTML lands on.
#
# Do NOT write `'none'` here: Tool documents are framed BY us, so `'none'`
# breaks every integrated Tool. (The `allow-scripts`-only sandbox gives the
# framed document an opaque origin, but `frame-ancestors` is matched
# against the ANCESTOR's origin, which is still the host.) Do NOT write
# `*`: that is precisely the third-party framing this issue denies.
# `scripts/check-frame-ancestors-policy.mjs` enforces both directions, and
# `test/integration/frame-ancestors.test.ts` proves the Worker half.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/tools/:slug/app/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'

/tools/:slug/app
  # The iframe document itself is fetched as a navigation (not a
  # module fetch), so it does not need CORS — but a permissive
  # `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` is harmless and keeps the rule
  # comprehensive for the whole Tool namespace.
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Caching — fingerprinted build assets (Issue #291)
#
# Baseline before this rule: Cloudflare Workers Static Assets serves
# every static file with `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`
# plus an `ETag`, so a repeat visit paid a full revalidation round trip
# for every JS/CSS chunk. Measured against `vite preview` (which applies
# this same `_headers` file):
#
#   GET /assets/index-B5Hb0lCH.css
#   cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate   <-- before
#   etag: "7b5cc500ffc7c1439a50578c25494f28"
#
# Vite emits everything under `/assets/` with
# `build.rollupOptions.output.assetFileNames` at its default
# `assets/[name]-[hash][extname]`, so the bytes behind any given
# `/assets/*` URL are immutable for the lifetime of that deployment —
# a new build produces a new hash and therefore a new URL. One year of
# freshness with `immutable` is safe and removes the revalidation round
# trip entirely; `immutable` also tells the browser not to revalidate
# even on reload.
#
# Deliberately NOT made immutable:
#   /share/**            stable, unhashed URLs with mutable contents.
#   /tools/:slug/app/**  Tool bundles keep stable entry URLs (index.html).
#   Both keep the Static Assets default `max-age=0, must-revalidate`,
#   which is the correct policy for a file whose URL outlives its bytes.
#
# 31556952 s = 52 weeks, the value Cloudflare documents as its own
# recommendation for fingerprinted assets.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31556952, immutable
